{"name":"Crossed Fingers","hex":"#ddb596","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crossed fingers","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Alternative spelling of fingers crossed (“hopefully, wishfully”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A superstitious hand gesture of good luck."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crossed_fingers"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crossed fingers","blocks":[{"text":"To cross one's fingers is a hand gesture commonly used to wish for luck or to bid one Godspeed. Early Christians used the gesture to implore the protection of the Holy Cross. The gesture is referred to by the common expressions \"cross your fingers\", \"keep your fingers crossed\", or just \"fingers crossed\". Historically, the gesture was accompanied by the words \"God spede you\" or \"God be with you.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Hands-Fingers-Crossed.jpg/330px-Hands-Fingers-Crossed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crossed_fingers","wikidata":"Q600807"},{"title":"Finger","blocks":[{"text":"A finger is a prominent, multi-jointed digit on the forelimbs of tetrapod vertebrate animals with prehensile extremities such as humans and other primates, and is typically covered with a keratinized nail to protect the end. Most tetrapods have five digits (pentadactyly), digits that are significantly shorter than the metacarpal/metatarsals, are typically referred to as toes; while digits that are notably elongated are called fingers. In humans, the fingers are opposable and have with two or three phalanges with fairly good flexional range of motion, serving as an important organ of tactile sensation and fine movements, which are crucial to the dexterity of the hands and the ability to grasp and manipulate objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Human_fingers_both_sides_2.jpg/330px-Human_fingers_both_sides_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finger","wikidata":"Q620207"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Beide_Daumen_dr%C3%BCcken.jpg/500px-Beide_Daumen_dr%C3%BCcken.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Beide_Daumen_dr%C3%BCcken.jpg/1280px-Beide_Daumen_dr%C3%BCcken.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Beide_Daumen_dr%C3%BCcken.jpg/500px-Beide_Daumen_dr%C3%BCcken.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pressing thumbs as gesture to wish for luck in German-speaking and many Slavic countries","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeide_Daumen_dr%C3%BCcken.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg/500px-Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg/1280px-Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg/500px-Syndactyly_type1_hands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Radiograph of Type 1 Syndactyly","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASyndactyly_type1_hands.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Fingers_of_a_treefrog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Fingers_of_a_treefrog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"ht